      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devon%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1881, merchants Lemuel Coffin and Joseph Altemus bought nearly 500 acres near the railroad station and founded Devon, opening the Devon Inn in 1882.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Devon,_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Devon Inn served as a hotel until 1914, after which it was repurposed and ultimately destroyed by fire in 1929.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Devon,_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Devon hosts the Devon Horse Show, the oldest and largest outdoor multi‑breed horse competition in the United States, held over ten days late May and early June.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devon%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community preserves 19th‑century architecture and sits just a short drive from Valley Forge National Historical Park.",
